package com.reps100.app;

import java.util.*;

public final class ExerciseInstructions {
  private static final Map<String,String> MAP = new HashMap<>();
  static {
    MAP.put("Bench Press", "1. Lie on a flat bench with eyes under the bar, feet planted, and shoulder blades pulled back and down.\n2. Grip the bar slightly wider than shoulder width so the forearms are vertical at the bottom.\n3. Unrack with straight wrists and lower the bar under control toward the lower half of the chest, keeping elbows roughly 45–70° from the torso.\n4. Touch the chest lightly without bouncing, then drive the bar up and slightly back until the elbows are straight.\n5. Keep the upper back, glutes and feet in contact with their supports throughout.\nCommon mistakes: bouncing the bar, wrists folding back, elbows flaring to 90°, or losing upper-back tension.");
    MAP.put("Incline Bench Press", "1. Set the bench to roughly 30–45° and position the eyes just behind the bar.\n2. Plant the feet, retract and depress the shoulder blades, and grip slightly wider than shoulder width.\n3. Lower the bar toward the upper chest/clavicle line with forearms close to vertical and elbows below the wrists.\n4. Press upward and slightly back until the arms are straight without shrugging the shoulders.\n5. Keep the torso against the bench; do not turn the movement into a steep overhead press.\nCommon mistakes: bench angle too high, bar drifting toward the stomach, elbows flaring, or lifting the hips.");
    MAP.put("Decline Bench Press", "1. Secure the legs in the decline bench and position the bar over the lower chest.\n2. Pull the shoulder blades back and down and use a grip slightly wider than shoulder width.\n3. Lower the bar toward the lower chest/upper abdomen while keeping wrists stacked over the forearms.\n4. Touch lightly, then press the bar vertically back to the start without bouncing.\n5. Keep the head, shoulders and hips supported throughout.\nCommon mistakes: lowering toward the neck, losing leg security, bouncing, or shortening the range.");
    MAP.put("Close-Grip Bench Press", "1. Lie on a flat bench with feet planted and shoulder blades set back and down.\n2. Grip the bar around shoulder width; do not use an excessively narrow grip.\n3. Lower toward the lower chest while keeping elbows closer to the ribs than in a standard bench press.\n4. Pause lightly at the chest, then extend the elbows to press the bar to arm’s length.\n5. Keep wrists neutral and shoulders packed.\nCommon mistakes: hands too close, elbows flaring, wrists collapsing, or bouncing the bar.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Bench Press", "1. Sit with one dumbbell on each thigh, then lie back and bring both dumbbells to chest level.\n2. Plant the feet and pull the shoulder blades back and down.\n3. Hold the dumbbells with wrists neutral and elbows about 45–60° from the torso.\n4. Lower both dumbbells until the elbows are just below bench level or a comfortable shoulder depth.\n5. Press both dumbbells upward and slightly inward until the arms are straight without clashing the dumbbells.\nCommon mistakes: dropping too deep, wrists bending back, uneven pressing, or losing shoulder-blade position.");
    MAP.put("Incline Dumbbell Press", "1. Set the bench to about 30–45° and sit with a dumbbell on each thigh.\n2. Kick the dumbbells into position beside the upper chest and set the shoulder blades back and down.\n3. Keep wrists stacked over elbows and lower the dumbbells until the upper arms are around bench level.\n4. Press upward and slightly inward over the upper chest until the elbows are straight.\n5. Keep the ribs controlled and avoid turning the press into a shoulder-dominant movement.\nCommon mistakes: bench too upright, over-arching, dumbbells drifting outward, or asymmetric range.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Fly", "1. Lie on a flat bench holding a dumbbell in each hand above the chest, palms facing each other.\n2. Keep a soft, fixed bend in the elbows and the shoulder blades gently retracted.\n3. Open the arms in a wide arc until a comfortable stretch is felt across the chest; the elbows should finish slightly below or level with the bench.\n4. Squeeze the chest to bring the dumbbells back along the same arc, stopping above the chest.\n5. Keep the elbow angle nearly unchanged throughout.\nCommon mistakes: turning the movement into a press, dropping too low, straightening the elbows, or shrugging.");
    MAP.put("Cable Fly", "1. Set both cable handles around chest height and step forward into a stable split stance.\n2. Start with arms open, elbows softly bent and hands slightly behind the chest line.\n3. Brace the torso and sweep the hands forward and inward in a broad arc.\n4. Bring the hands together in front of the sternum without letting the shoulders roll forward.\n5. Return slowly until the chest is stretched while keeping the same elbow angle.\nCommon mistakes: excessive torso lean, bending and straightening the elbows, or letting the shoulders protract at the finish.");
    MAP.put("Machine Chest Press", "1. Adjust the seat so the handles line up with the mid-chest and the feet are flat on the floor.\n2. Sit back with shoulder blades against the pad and wrists directly behind the handles.\n3. Press the handles forward until the elbows are straight but not forcefully locked.\n4. Return under control until the elbows move just behind the torso or to the machine’s comfortable stop.\n5. Keep the shoulders down and the head against the pad.\nCommon mistakes: seat too high/low, shrugging, elbows flaring excessively, or allowing the stack to slam.");
    MAP.put("Lat Pulldown", "1. Adjust the thigh pad so the legs are secure and take an overhand grip slightly wider than shoulder width.\n2. Sit tall with a small backward lean, chest lifted and ribs controlled.\n3. Start by drawing the shoulder blades down, then pull the elbows toward the sides of the ribs.\n4. Bring the bar to the upper chest without pulling behind the neck.\n5. Extend the arms slowly until the lats are fully lengthened while keeping the torso stable.\nCommon mistakes: rocking backward, pulling behind the neck, using momentum, or stopping short at the top.");
    MAP.put("Straight-Arm Pulldown", "1. Set a straight bar or rope on a high pulley and stand far enough back for cable tension with arms nearly straight.\n2. Hinge slightly at the hips, brace the abdomen and keep a small fixed bend in the elbows.\n3. Drive the hands down in an arc toward the thighs by extending the shoulders.\n4. Pause when the hands reach the thighs and the lats are shortened.\n5. Return overhead under control without allowing the ribs to flare.\nCommon mistakes: turning it into a triceps press, excessive torso movement, or bending the elbows significantly.");
    MAP.put("Barbell Row", "1. Hold the bar just outside shoulder width, hinge at the hips and keep the torso roughly 30–45° above horizontal.\n2. Brace the spine neutral, knees softly bent, and let the arms hang straight.\n3. Pull the bar toward the lower ribs/upper abdomen while driving the elbows behind the body.\n4. Squeeze the shoulder blades without shrugging, then lower until the elbows are fully straight.\n5. Keep the torso angle stable throughout.\nCommon mistakes: jerking with the hips, rounding the back, pulling to the chest, or shortening the bottom position.");
    MAP.put("Pendlay Row", "1. Start each rep with the bar resting on the floor, feet about hip width and torso close to parallel with the floor.\n2. Grip slightly wider than shoulder width, brace hard and keep the back flat.\n3. Pull explosively from the floor toward the lower chest/upper abdomen without changing torso angle.\n4. Lower the bar back to the floor under control and let it settle before the next rep.\n5. Re-brace before every repetition.\nCommon mistakes: using a high torso angle, bouncing the bar, rounding the back, or turning it into a conventional bent-over row.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Row", "1. Support one hand and knee on a bench, with the working foot planted and spine neutral.\n2. Let the dumbbell hang directly below the shoulder with the arm long.\n3. Pull the elbow back toward the hip, keeping the shoulder away from the ear.\n4. Stop when the upper arm reaches the torso without twisting the chest upward.\n5. Lower until the shoulder blade can move forward slightly while the back stays neutral.\nCommon mistakes: rotating the torso, shrugging, curling the dumbbell, or using momentum.");
    MAP.put("Chest-Supported Row", "1. Set an incline bench so the chest is fully supported while the arms can hang freely.\n2. Hold the dumbbells or handles with shoulders relaxed and spine neutral against the pad.\n3. Pull the elbows back toward the ribs, choosing a lower elbow path for lats or slightly wider for upper back.\n4. Pause with the shoulder blades drawn together without lifting the chest from the pad.\n5. Lower until the arms are straight and the shoulder blades can protract slightly.\nCommon mistakes: lifting the torso off the pad, shrugging, jerking, or shortening the stretch.");
    MAP.put("Seated Cable Row", "1. Sit tall with feet braced, knees softly bent and the cable handle at lower-chest height.\n2. Start with arms straight and a neutral spine; allow a small controlled reach from the shoulder blades.\n3. Pull the handle toward the lower ribs while driving the elbows backward.\n4. Finish with the chest tall and shoulders down, not by leaning far behind vertical.\n5. Extend the arms under control and allow the shoulder blades to move forward without rounding the low back.\nCommon mistakes: excessive rocking, shrugging, pulling too high, or using the lower back to initiate the rep.");
    MAP.put("Face Pull", "1. Set a rope on a high pulley around eye to forehead height and take an overhand/neutral grip.\n2. Step back with arms straight, ribs down and shoulders relaxed.\n3. Pull the rope toward the face while separating the hands and driving elbows out and back.\n4. Finish with hands beside the ears and forearms roughly vertical, externally rotating the shoulders.\n5. Return slowly until the arms are straight.\nCommon mistakes: cable set too low, leaning back excessively, shrugging, or pulling only with the elbows.");
    MAP.put("Back Squat", "1. Position the bar across the upper traps/rear shoulders, grip firmly and stand with feet about shoulder width.\n2. Brace the abdomen, keep the whole foot planted and turn the toes slightly outward.\n3. Sit the hips down between the heels while the knees track in line with the toes.\n4. Descend to at least thigh-parallel if mobility and control allow, keeping the bar balanced over mid-foot.\n5. Drive the floor away to stand, extending knees and hips together.\nCommon mistakes: heels lifting, knees collapsing inward, losing spinal position, or hips shooting up before the chest.");
    MAP.put("Front Squat", "1. Rack the bar across the front deltoids with elbows high, using a clean grip or crossed-arm grip.\n2. Stand around shoulder width, brace and keep the chest tall.\n3. Descend mostly straight down while the knees travel forward over the toes and the heels stay planted.\n4. Keep the elbows pointing forward so the bar stays over mid-foot.\n5. Drive upward through the whole foot and finish tall.\nCommon mistakes: elbows dropping, torso folding forward, heels lifting, or knees collapsing inward.");
    MAP.put("Goblet Squat", "1. Hold the weight vertically against the upper chest with both hands and elbows angled down.\n2. Stand slightly wider than hip width with toes turned out a little.\n3. Brace, then sit the hips down between the legs while the knees follow the toes.\n4. Descend until the thighs are at least near parallel or your controlled depth.\n5. Push through the whole foot to stand while keeping the weight close to the chest.\nCommon mistakes: weight drifting away, heels lifting, knees collapsing inward, or rounding the lower back.");
    MAP.put("Kettlebell Goblet Squat", "1. Hold the weight vertically against the upper chest with both hands and elbows angled down.\n2. Stand slightly wider than hip width with toes turned out a little.\n3. Brace, then sit the hips down between the legs while the knees follow the toes.\n4. Descend until the thighs are at least near parallel or your controlled depth.\n5. Push through the whole foot to stand while keeping the weight close to the chest.\nCommon mistakes: weight drifting away, heels lifting, knees collapsing inward, or rounding the lower back.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Squat", "1. Hold a dumbbell in each hand at the sides and stand around shoulder width.\n2. Brace the torso, keep the shoulders down and distribute pressure across the whole foot.\n3. Bend the knees and hips together, allowing the dumbbells to travel vertically beside the legs.\n4. Descend to a comfortable squat depth while knees track over toes.\n5. Stand by driving through mid-foot and heel, keeping both dumbbells steady.\nCommon mistakes: leaning forward excessively, dumbbells swinging, knees collapsing inward, or heels lifting.");
    MAP.put("Hack Squat", "1. Place the back and shoulders firmly against the machine pads and position feet shoulder width on the platform.\n2. Release the safeties and start with knees and hips straight but not hyperextended.\n3. Lower the sled by bending hips and knees while keeping the back against the pad.\n4. Descend until thighs are around parallel or to a comfortable controlled depth.\n5. Push through the whole foot to extend the knees and hips, then reset the safeties at the end.\nCommon mistakes: feet too low causing heels to lift, knees collapsing inward, bouncing at the bottom, or locking the knees aggressively.");
    MAP.put("Leg Press", "1. Sit with the pelvis and lower back supported and place the feet shoulder width on the platform.\n2. Release the safeties and lower the platform by bending the knees toward the chest.\n3. Keep the heels planted and stop before the pelvis rolls away from the back pad.\n4. Press through the whole foot until the legs are nearly straight.\n5. Keep knees tracking with the toes throughout.\nCommon mistakes: excessive depth that rounds the pelvis, knees collapsing inward, heels lifting, or forcefully locking the knees.");
    MAP.put("Leg Extension", "1. Adjust the machine so the knee joint lines up with the machine pivot and the pad rests above the ankles.\n2. Sit back with hips against the pad and hold the handles.\n3. Extend the knees until the legs are nearly straight, keeping the thighs on the seat.\n4. Pause briefly at the top without kicking or snapping the knees.\n5. Lower the pad slowly until the knees are comfortably flexed.\nCommon mistakes: seat misalignment, swinging the torso, using momentum, or slamming the weight stack.");
    MAP.put("Reverse Lunge", "1. Stand tall with feet hip width and brace the trunk.\n2. Step one foot backward far enough that both knees can bend comfortably.\n3. Lower the rear knee toward the floor while keeping most pressure through the front mid-foot and heel.\n4. Keep the front knee tracking over the middle toes and the torso upright.\n5. Push through the front foot to return to standing, then repeat on the other side as programmed.\nCommon mistakes: stepping too narrow, front heel lifting, front knee collapsing inward, or pushing excessively from the rear foot.");
    MAP.put("Bodyweight Reverse Lunge", "1. Stand tall with feet hip width and brace the trunk.\n2. Step one foot backward far enough that both knees can bend comfortably.\n3. Lower the rear knee toward the floor while keeping most pressure through the front mid-foot and heel.\n4. Keep the front knee tracking over the middle toes and the torso upright.\n5. Push through the front foot to return to standing, then repeat on the other side as programmed.\nCommon mistakes: stepping too narrow, front heel lifting, front knee collapsing inward, or pushing excessively from the rear foot.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Lunges", "1. Hold a dumbbell in each hand at the sides with shoulders down and torso tall.\n2. Step forward into a long stance and plant the whole front foot.\n3. Lower the rear knee toward the floor until both knees are roughly 90°, keeping the front knee tracking over the toes.\n4. Drive through the front heel/mid-foot to bring the rear leg forward into the next step.\n5. Keep the dumbbells quiet beside the body and pelvis level.\nCommon mistakes: taking steps that are too short, wobbling side to side, front heel lifting, or using the rear leg to push off excessively.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Romanian Deadlift", "1. Hold a dumbbell in each hand in front of the thighs, feet hip width and knees softly bent.\n2. Brace and push the hips backward while sliding the dumbbells close down the thighs and shins.\n3. Keep the spine neutral and shoulders packed as the torso inclines forward.\n4. Stop when hamstring tension limits further hip travel, usually around mid-shin.\n5. Drive the hips forward to stand, keeping the dumbbells close.\nCommon mistakes: squatting the movement, rounding the back, letting dumbbells drift forward, or leaning back at the top.");
    MAP.put("Romanian Deadlift", "1. Hold the barbell in front of the thighs, feet hip width and knees softly bent.\n2. Brace and push the hips backward while sliding the bar close down the thighs and shins.\n3. Keep the spine neutral and shoulders packed as the torso inclines forward.\n4. Stop when hamstring tension limits further hip travel, usually around mid-shin.\n5. Drive the hips forward to stand, keeping the bar close.\nCommon mistakes: squatting the movement, rounding the back, letting bar drift forward, or leaning back at the top.");
    MAP.put("Conventional Deadlift", "1. Stand with mid-foot under the bar, feet about hip width, and grip the bar just outside the legs.\n2. Bring the shins to the bar, set the hips between a squat and hinge position, lift the chest and brace hard.\n3. Push the floor away while keeping the bar close to the shins and thighs.\n4. Extend knees and hips together and finish standing tall without leaning backward.\n5. Return by pushing the hips back first, then bend the knees once the bar passes them.\nCommon mistakes: bar starting too far forward, hips shooting up early, rounded back, or jerking the bar from the floor.");
    MAP.put("Sumo Deadlift", "1. Take a wide stance with toes turned outward and shins close to the bar.\n2. Grip the bar inside the knees, brace, spread the floor with the feet and keep the chest tall.\n3. Push through the floor as the knees track outward, keeping the bar close and torso more upright than a conventional deadlift.\n4. Lock out by extending hips and knees together without leaning back.\n5. Lower under control by hinging then bending the knees.\nCommon mistakes: knees collapsing inward, stance too wide for mobility, hips rising first, or bar drifting forward.");
    MAP.put("Trap Bar Deadlift", "1. Stand centred inside the trap bar with feet about hip width and handles beside the legs.\n2. Hinge and bend the knees to grip the handles, brace and keep the chest tall.\n3. Drive through the whole foot while extending knees and hips together.\n4. Stand tall with shoulders over hips, then lower by pushing the hips back and bending the knees.\n5. Keep the handles level and the torso stable.\nCommon mistakes: starting off-centre, knees collapsing inward, shrugging during the lift, or overextending the low back.");
    MAP.put("Good Morning", "1. Place a light bar securely across the upper back as for a back squat and stand hip to shoulder width.\n2. Unlock the knees slightly, brace the abdomen and keep the upper back tight.\n3. Push the hips backward while the torso inclines forward and shins remain nearly vertical.\n4. Stop when hamstring tension is strong but the spine is still neutral.\n5. Drive the hips forward to return upright.\nCommon mistakes: bending the knees into a squat, rounding the back, using too much load, or descending beyond hamstring mobility.");
    MAP.put("Lying Leg Curl", "1. Adjust the machine so the knee joint aligns with its pivot and the roller sits just above the heels.\n2. Lie flat with hips pressed into the pad and brace the abdomen.\n3. Curl the heels toward the glutes by bending the knees without lifting the hips.\n4. Pause briefly at peak knee flexion, then lower slowly until the legs are almost straight.\n5. Keep both legs moving evenly.\nCommon mistakes: hips lifting, bouncing the stack, excessive lumbar arch, or shortening the bottom range.");
    MAP.put("Seated Leg Curl", "1. Align the knees with the machine pivot and secure the thigh pad just above the knees.\n2. Place the lower-leg pad above the heels and sit tall with the back supported.\n3. Curl the heels down and back by flexing the knees as far as comfortably possible.\n4. Pause briefly, then return slowly until the hamstrings are lengthened.\n5. Keep the hips and thighs firmly against the pads.\nCommon mistakes: hips sliding forward, torso rocking, using momentum, or letting the weight stack crash.");
    MAP.put("Single-Leg Glute Bridge", "1. Lie on the back with one foot flat near the glute and the other leg lifted.\n2. Brace the abdomen and lightly tuck the pelvis so the ribs stay down.\n3. Drive through the planted heel and extend the hip until shoulder, hip and knee form a line.\n4. Keep the pelvis level and squeeze the working glute at the top.\n5. Lower under control without rotating the hips.\nCommon mistakes: pushing through the toes, arching the low back, pelvis twisting, or using the raised leg for momentum.");
    MAP.put("Cable Kickback", "1. Attach an ankle cuff to a low cable and stand facing the machine while holding it for balance.\n2. Keep the support knee soft, torso tall or slightly hinged, and pelvis square.\n3. With the working knee slightly bent, drive the leg backward from the hip without rotating the pelvis.\n4. Stop when the glute is fully contracted before the low back begins to arch.\n5. Return slowly until the hip is flexed slightly.\nCommon mistakes: swinging the leg, arching the back, turning the foot outward, or bending the knee excessively.");
    MAP.put("Hip Abduction", "1. Sit in the machine with the back supported and pads against the outside of the thighs.\n2. Set feet securely and brace the torso.\n3. Press the knees outward by moving from the hips, keeping both sides controlled.\n4. Pause at the widest comfortable position.\n5. Return slowly until the pads nearly meet.\nCommon mistakes: bouncing, leaning dramatically, allowing the stack to slam, or using a range that causes hip pinching.");
    MAP.put("Standing Calf Raise", "1. Stand with the balls of the feet on the platform and the bar/load supported securely.\n2. Keep knees nearly straight and ankles aligned, allowing the heels to lower below the forefoot if the setup permits.\n3. Press through the balls of the feet to rise as high as possible onto the toes.\n4. Pause briefly at the top without rolling onto the outer edges of the feet.\n5. Lower slowly to a full calf stretch.\nCommon mistakes: bouncing, bending the knees, using partial reps, or letting ankles roll outward.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Calf Raise", "1. Hold a dumbbell in each hand and stand with the balls of the feet on a stable edge or flat floor.\n2. Keep the knees nearly straight and use support for balance if needed.\n3. Lower the heels under control to stretch the calves.\n4. Press through the big-toe side of the forefoot and rise onto the toes.\n5. Pause at the top, then lower slowly.\nCommon mistakes: bouncing, rushing the bottom stretch, ankles rolling outward, or bending the knees to generate momentum.");
    MAP.put("Overhead Press", "1. Rack the bar at upper-chest height with hands just outside shoulder width and forearms nearly vertical.\n2. Stand with feet about hip width, squeeze the glutes and brace the abdomen.\n3. Move the head slightly back and press the bar vertically from the upper chest.\n4. As the bar clears the forehead, move the head through so the bar finishes stacked over shoulders and mid-foot.\n5. Lower to the upper chest under control.\nCommon mistakes: excessive back arch, pressing around the face, wrists bent back, or bar finishing in front of the body.");
    MAP.put("Arnold Press", "1. Sit or stand tall holding a dumbbell in each hand in front of the shoulders, palms facing you.\n2. Brace the torso and begin rotating the palms outward as the elbows move laterally.\n3. Continue pressing overhead until the arms are straight and palms face forward.\n4. Reverse the same path, rotating inward as the dumbbells return in front of the shoulders.\n5. Keep the movement smooth and symmetrical.\nCommon mistakes: excessive lower-back arch, rushing the rotation, dropping elbows too low, or using momentum.");
    MAP.put("Cable Lateral Raise", "1. Set a single cable low and stand side-on, holding the handle with the outside arm or across the body.\n2. Keep a slight elbow bend, wrist neutral and shoulder down.\n3. Raise the arm out to the side in the scapular plane until around shoulder height.\n4. Lead with the elbow rather than shrugging the shoulder.\n5. Lower slowly across the body until the deltoid is stretched.\nCommon mistakes: leaning excessively, swinging, raising far above shoulder height, or internally rotating the arm aggressively.");
    MAP.put("Front Raise", "1. Hold the dumbbells in front of the thighs with a slight bend in the elbows.\n2. Stand tall, brace and keep the shoulders down.\n3. Raise one or both arms forward until around shoulder height while maintaining the same elbow angle.\n4. Pause briefly, then lower under control to the thighs.\n5. Keep the torso still.\nCommon mistakes: leaning backward, swinging, shrugging, or raising far above shoulder height.");
    MAP.put("Rear Delt Fly", "1. Hinge at the hips until the torso is inclined forward, spine neutral and knees softly bent.\n2. Let the dumbbells hang beneath the shoulders with elbows slightly bent.\n3. Sweep the arms out and slightly back until roughly in line with the shoulders.\n4. Keep the shoulders down and squeeze the rear delts/upper back without shrugging.\n5. Lower slowly until the arms hang again.\nCommon mistakes: using too much weight, straightening and bending elbows, torso swinging, or pulling the dumbbells toward the hips.");
    MAP.put("Reverse Pec Deck", "1. Adjust the seat so the handles are around shoulder height and sit facing the pad with chest supported.\n2. Grasp the handles with a neutral or pronated grip and keep elbows softly bent.\n3. Open the arms outward and back in a wide arc until they are roughly in line with the torso.\n4. Keep shoulders down and avoid arching away from the pad.\n5. Return slowly until the rear delts are stretched.\nCommon mistakes: seat height wrong, shrugging, bouncing, or squeezing so far back that the shoulders roll forward.");
    MAP.put("Upright Row", "1. Hold the bar around shoulder width in front of the thighs and stand tall.\n2. Brace and pull the bar upward close to the body by leading with the elbows.\n3. Stop when the elbows reach roughly shoulder height or earlier if the shoulders feel restricted.\n4. Keep wrists below or level with the elbows and shoulders down.\n5. Lower under control to the thighs.\nCommon mistakes: very narrow grip, pulling excessively high, shrugging, or swinging the torso.");
    MAP.put("Barbell Curl", "1. Stand tall holding the bar around shoulder width with elbows close to the ribs.\n2. Keep the wrists neutral and upper arms stationary.\n3. Flex the elbows to bring the bar toward the shoulders without leaning backward.\n4. Squeeze briefly near the top, then lower until the elbows are nearly straight.\n5. For reverse curls use an overhand grip and keep the wrists from dropping.\nCommon mistakes: torso swing, elbows drifting forward, wrists flexing, or dropping the bar quickly.");
    MAP.put("Reverse Curl", "1. Stand tall holding the bar around shoulder width with elbows close to the ribs.\n2. Keep the wrists neutral and upper arms stationary.\n3. Flex the elbows to bring the bar toward the shoulders without leaning backward.\n4. Squeeze briefly near the top, then lower until the elbows are nearly straight.\n5. For reverse curls use an overhand grip and keep the wrists from dropping.\nCommon mistakes: torso swing, elbows drifting forward, wrists flexing, or dropping the bar quickly.");
    MAP.put("Hammer Curl", "1. Hold a dumbbell in each hand with palms facing the thighs and elbows close to the ribs.\n2. Curl the dumbbells upward while maintaining the neutral, thumbs-up grip.\n3. Keep the upper arms still and wrists straight.\n4. Stop when the forearms approach vertical, then lower slowly to full elbow extension.\n5. Keep both shoulders down.\nCommon mistakes: swinging, elbows travelling forward, wrist bending, or using shortened reps.");
    MAP.put("Incline Dumbbell Curl", "1. Set an incline bench around 45–60° and sit with the arms hanging behind the torso.\n2. Hold a dumbbell in each hand with palms forward and shoulders relaxed.\n3. Curl without allowing the elbows to move forward.\n4. Squeeze at the top, then lower slowly until the biceps are fully lengthened.\n5. Keep the back against the bench.\nCommon mistakes: lifting the elbows, shrugging, swinging, or stopping before full extension.");
    MAP.put("Preacher Curl", "1. Adjust the seat so the armpits rest near the top of the preacher pad and upper arms lie flat on it.\n2. Hold the bar or dumbbells with wrists neutral and begin with elbows almost straight.\n3. Curl toward the shoulders while keeping the upper arms in contact with the pad.\n4. Stop before the forearms become fully vertical if tension disappears.\n5. Lower slowly to a long-arm position without hyperextending the elbows.\nCommon mistakes: lifting off the pad, bouncing at the bottom, bending wrists, or using momentum.");
    MAP.put("Concentration Curl", "1. Sit with feet wide and brace the working upper arm against the inside of the thigh.\n2. Let the dumbbell hang with the elbow nearly straight and wrist neutral.\n3. Curl toward the shoulder while the upper arm stays fixed against the thigh.\n4. Squeeze the biceps, then lower slowly to full length.\n5. Keep the torso still.\nCommon mistakes: shoulder movement, wrist curling, bouncing, or shortening the bottom range.");
    MAP.put("Cable Curl", "1. Set a straight or EZ attachment on a low pulley and stand tall facing the stack.\n2. Hold the handle around shoulder width with elbows tucked near the ribs.\n3. Curl the handle toward the shoulders while keeping upper arms stationary.\n4. Pause briefly, then extend the elbows slowly until the biceps are lengthened.\n5. Keep cable tension and torso position constant.\nCommon mistakes: leaning back, elbows moving forward, wrists bending, or letting the stack slam.");
    MAP.put("Triceps Pushdown", "1. Set a bar attachment on a high pulley and stand close to the cable with elbows pinned near the ribs.\n2. Start with elbows flexed about 90° and wrists neutral.\n3. Extend the elbows to drive the handle down toward the thighs without moving the upper arms.\n4. Straighten fully and squeeze the triceps without rounding the shoulders.\n5. Return until the forearms are just above parallel while elbows remain tucked.\nCommon mistakes: shoulders rocking, elbows drifting forward, torso bouncing, or incomplete lockout.");
    MAP.put("Overhead Triceps Extension", "1. Set a rope on a high or low cable and face away from the stack with a stable split stance.\n2. Position the elbows beside the head and allow them to bend so the hands move behind the head.\n3. Keep the upper arms mostly fixed and extend the elbows until the arms are straight.\n4. Maintain ribs down and avoid arching the low back.\n5. Return slowly to a deep but comfortable triceps stretch.\nCommon mistakes: elbows flaring excessively, back arching, upper arms moving, or cutting the stretch short.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Skullcrusher", "1. Lie on a bench holding one dumbbell in each hand above the shoulders with palms facing inward.\n2. Keep the upper arms nearly vertical and shoulder blades stable.\n3. Bend only the elbows to lower the dumbbells beside the head.\n4. Stop at a comfortable triceps stretch, then extend the elbows to return the dumbbells over the shoulders.\n5. Keep wrists neutral and upper arms quiet.\nCommon mistakes: elbows drifting wide, shoulders turning it into a pullover, dropping too deep, or bouncing.");
    MAP.put("Bicycle Crunch", "1. Lie on the back with hips and knees bent, fingertips lightly behind the head.\n2. Curl the shoulder blades off the floor and extend one leg while rotating the ribcage toward the opposite knee.\n3. Bring the opposite elbow and knee toward each other without pulling the neck.\n4. Switch sides smoothly while the low back stays gently pressed toward the floor.\n5. Count one left-plus-right cycle or each side consistently.\nCommon mistakes: yanking the head, pedalling too fast, low back arching, or moving only the elbows.");
    MAP.put("Reverse Crunch", "1. Lie on the back with hips and knees around 90° and arms beside the body.\n2. Brace the abdomen and gently flatten the low back.\n3. Curl the pelvis toward the ribs so the tailbone lifts a few centimetres from the floor.\n4. Keep the knees bent and avoid swinging the legs.\n5. Lower the pelvis slowly until the hips return to the start.\nCommon mistakes: throwing the legs overhead, using momentum, or arching the back between reps.");
    MAP.put("Sit-Up", "1. Lie on the back with knees bent, feet planted and arms crossed over the chest or hands lightly at the temples.\n2. Brace the abdomen and begin by curling the ribs toward the pelvis.\n3. Continue lifting the torso until upright or to the chosen controlled range.\n4. Keep the feet planted and neck neutral.\n5. Lower the spine back to the floor under control.\nCommon mistakes: pulling on the neck, feet lifting, bouncing off the floor, or dropping quickly.");
    MAP.put("Dead Bug", "1. Lie on the back with hips and knees at 90° and arms reaching toward the ceiling.\n2. Exhale and gently press the low back toward the floor.\n3. Slowly extend one leg and the opposite arm away from the body without letting the back arch.\n4. Return to the start and repeat on the other side.\n5. Move only as far as trunk control allows.\nCommon mistakes: low back lifting, rushing, holding the breath, or extending farther than control permits.");
    MAP.put("Hanging Knee Raise", "1. Hang from a bar with shoulders active and body still.\n2. Brace the abdomen and tuck the pelvis slightly.\n3. Bend the knees and lift them toward the chest by flexing the hips and curling the pelvis.\n4. Pause without swinging, then lower until the hips are extended and legs hang still.\n5. Reset before the next rep.\nCommon mistakes: kipping, only flexing the hips without pelvic curl, shrugging, or dropping the legs.");
    MAP.put("Hanging Leg Raise", "1. Hang from a bar with straight legs, shoulders active and body still.\n2. Brace the abdomen and begin by tilting the pelvis backward.\n3. Lift the straight legs forward toward horizontal or higher while avoiding swing.\n4. Pause at the top, then lower under control to a dead-still hang.\n5. Bend the knees slightly if hamstring flexibility limits the range.\nCommon mistakes: kipping, excessive shoulder shrug, low-back arching, or uncontrolled descent.");
    MAP.put("Ab Wheel Rollout", "1. Kneel with the wheel beneath the shoulders, hands firm and pelvis slightly tucked.\n2. Brace the glutes and abdomen so the ribs stay down.\n3. Roll forward by extending the shoulders and hips together while keeping a long line from knees to head.\n4. Stop before the low back sags, then pull the wheel back by engaging the lats and abdomen.\n5. Finish over the knees without sitting back excessively.\nCommon mistakes: low-back extension, bending at the hips too early, rolling beyond control, or pulling only with the arms.");
    MAP.put("Cable Wood Chop", "1. Set the cable high or low depending on the desired diagonal and stand side-on with feet stable.\n2. Hold the handle with both hands and brace the pelvis and ribcage.\n3. Rotate through the torso and hips while guiding the hands diagonally across the body.\n4. Keep the arms relatively long and allow the back foot to pivot if needed.\n5. Return slowly against the cable tension.\nCommon mistakes: pulling only with the arms, knees collapsing, excessive lumbar twisting, or rushing the return.");
    MAP.put("Kettlebell Swing", "1. Place the kettlebell slightly in front, stand wider than hip width and hinge to grip it with both hands.\n2. Hike the bell back between the thighs with a neutral spine and shins nearly vertical.\n3. Snap the hips forward powerfully; let the bell float to about chest height without lifting with the arms.\n4. Allow the bell to fall, then hinge late and guide it high between the thighs.\n5. Repeat with a rhythmic hinge and finish by parking the bell safely.\nCommon mistakes: squatting the swing, lifting with the shoulders, hyperextending the back, or letting the bell pull the chest down.");
    MAP.put("Kettlebell Clean", "1. Start with the kettlebell between the feet or slightly forward and hinge to grip it with one hand.\n2. Drive through the hips to accelerate the bell close to the body.\n3. Keep the elbow close to the ribs and guide the hand around the bell rather than letting it flip over the wrist.\n4. Finish in the rack position with wrist neutral, forearm vertical-ish and bell resting softly on the forearm.\n5. Reverse under control to the hinge for the next rep.\nCommon mistakes: swinging the bell far away, smashing the forearm, curling with the arm, or overextending the back.");
    MAP.put("Kettlebell Snatch", "1. Hike the kettlebell back with one hand from a strong hip hinge.\n2. Drive the hips and guide the bell upward close to the body.\n3. As it passes head height, punch the hand through the handle so the bell wraps smoothly around the wrist.\n4. Finish overhead with elbow locked, shoulder stable and ribs down.\n5. Guide the bell back through the rack or directly into the backswing under control.\nCommon mistakes: bell looping far forward, forearm impact, pressing the bell out, or losing trunk control overhead.");
    MAP.put("Turkish Get-Up", "1. Lie on the back holding the kettlebell locked out over one shoulder; bend the same-side knee and plant that foot.\n2. Roll to the opposite elbow, then hand, keeping eyes on the bell and shoulder packed.\n3. Bridge the hips and sweep the straight leg underneath into a half-kneeling position.\n4. Square the torso and stand while the kettlebell remains vertically stacked overhead.\n5. Reverse every step slowly to return to the floor.\nCommon mistakes: bending the loaded elbow, rushing transitions, losing wrist/shoulder stacking, or taking eyes off the bell too early.");
    MAP.put("Kettlebell Thruster", "1. Hold one or two kettlebells in the rack position with feet around shoulder width.\n2. Brace and squat down while keeping the chest tall and knees tracking over toes.\n3. Drive powerfully out of the squat and transfer that momentum into an overhead press.\n4. Finish with the bell(s) stacked over the shoulders and ribs controlled.\n5. Lower to rack before beginning the next squat.\nCommon mistakes: pressing before the legs drive, heels lifting, back arching overhead, or letting the rack collapse.");
    MAP.put("Dumbbell Thruster", "1. Hold a dumbbell in each hand at shoulder height with feet around shoulder width.\n2. Squat with knees tracking over toes and the dumbbells balanced over the shoulders.\n3. Drive hard through the legs and continue directly into an overhead press.\n4. Finish with both dumbbells over the shoulders, elbows straight and ribs down.\n5. Lower the dumbbells back to shoulder level before the next squat.\nCommon mistakes: pressing too early, knees collapsing, losing heel contact, or over-arching overhead.");
    MAP.put("High Knees", "1. Stand tall with feet under the hips and arms bent as for running.\n2. Run in place, driving one knee upward toward hip height while the opposite arm swings forward.\n3. Land lightly on the ball/mid-foot beneath the body and switch legs quickly.\n4. Keep the torso tall and cadence even.\n5. Count each knee drive as a rep if using repetitions.\nCommon mistakes: leaning far backward, heavy heel strikes, low knee drive, or losing rhythm.");
    MAP.put("Box Jump", "1. Stand about one foot-length from a stable box with feet around hip width.\n2. Swing the arms and dip into a shallow squat to load the hips.\n3. Jump upward and forward, driving the arms and extending hips, knees and ankles.\n4. Land softly with both feet fully on the box, knees tracking over toes and hips flexed.\n5. Stand tall, then step down rather than jumping down when fatigue is high.\nCommon mistakes: box too high, knees collapsing on landing, landing on the toes only, or repeatedly jumping down under fatigue.");
    MAP.put("Bodyweight Sumo Squat", "1. Stand wider than shoulder width with toes turned outward about 30–45°.\n2. Brace the torso and keep the whole foot planted.\n3. Lower the hips straight down between the legs while pushing the knees in the same direction as the toes.\n4. Descend until thighs are near parallel or to a comfortable depth.\n5. Drive through the feet and squeeze the glutes to stand.\nCommon mistakes: knees collapsing inward, stance wider than hip mobility allows, heels lifting, or torso folding forward.");
    MAP.put("Bodyweight Side Lunge", "1. Stand tall with feet together or hip width.\n2. Step one foot well out to the side, keeping both feet pointing mostly forward.\n3. Sit the hips back over the stepping leg while that knee bends and the opposite leg stays straight.\n4. Keep the working foot flat and knee tracking over the toes.\n5. Push through the working foot to return to centre.\nCommon mistakes: knee collapsing inward, heel lifting, torso rounding, or turning it into a forward lunge.");
    MAP.put("Single Leg Squat", "1. Stand on one leg with the free leg held forward and arms extended for balance.\n2. Brace and sit the hips down and back while the working knee tracks over the toes.\n3. Descend only as far as balance, ankle mobility and knee control permit.\n4. Keep the heel down and pelvis as level as possible.\n5. Drive through the whole foot to stand without the free foot touching down.\nCommon mistakes: knee collapsing inward, heel lifting, pelvis twisting, or dropping rapidly into the bottom.");
    MAP.put("Squat Jump", "1. Stand around shoulder width and descend into a shallow-to-moderate squat.\n2. Swing the arms back and load the hips while keeping knees aligned with toes.\n3. Explosively extend hips, knees and ankles to jump vertically.\n4. Land softly on both feet with knees and hips flexed to absorb impact.\n5. Reset posture before the next rep rather than rebounding if control is lost.\nCommon mistakes: knees collapsing on take-off/landing, stiff landings, excessive depth before every jump, or continuing when jump height drops sharply.");
    MAP.put("Squat Jumps", "1. Stand around shoulder width and descend into a shallow-to-moderate squat.\n2. Swing the arms back and load the hips while keeping knees aligned with toes.\n3. Explosively extend hips, knees and ankles to jump vertically.\n4. Land softly on both feet with knees and hips flexed to absorb impact.\n5. Reset posture before the next rep rather than rebounding if control is lost.\nCommon mistakes: knees collapsing on take-off/landing, stiff landings, excessive depth before every jump, or continuing when jump height drops sharply.");
    MAP.put("Side-to-Side Jump", "1. Stand with knees and hips slightly flexed and weight balanced over the mid-foot.\n2. Push off one leg to jump laterally to the opposite side.\n3. Land softly on the opposite foot or both feet as programmed, allowing hip and knee flexion.\n4. Stabilise briefly with the knee tracking over the toes.\n5. Reverse direction for the next rep.\nCommon mistakes: knee collapsing inward, landing on a stiff leg, crossing the feet, or jumping farther than balance allows.");
    MAP.put("Forward-Back Jump", "1. Stand in an athletic stance with feet hip width and knees softly bent.\n2. Jump forward a short controlled distance using both feet.\n3. Land softly with hips and knees flexed and weight centred.\n4. Jump backward to the starting position using the same controlled landing.\n5. Keep each jump quick but quiet and stable.\nCommon mistakes: stiff knees, landing on heels, excessive forward lean, or increasing distance beyond control.");
    MAP.put("Bodyweight Step-Up", "1. Face a stable step or box high enough to challenge the leg without forcing the pelvis to tilt.\n2. Place the whole working foot on the box and lean the torso slightly forward.\n3. Drive through that foot to extend the hip and knee, bringing the body onto the box.\n4. Avoid pushing strongly from the trailing foot.\n5. Lower under control by bending the working leg and return the trailing foot to the floor.\nCommon mistakes: only the toes on the box, pushing off the rear leg, knee collapsing inward, or dropping quickly.");
    MAP.put("Donkey Kicks", "1. Start on hands and knees with wrists under shoulders and knees under hips.\n2. Brace the abdomen and keep the spine neutral.\n3. Keeping the working knee bent about 90°, drive the sole of the foot upward/backward by extending the hip.\n4. Stop before the low back arches or pelvis rotates.\n5. Lower the knee under control without resting fully between reps.\nCommon mistakes: lumbar arching, rotating the pelvis, swinging the leg, or trying to lift the foot excessively high.");
    MAP.put("Bodyweight Kickbacks", "1. Start on hands and knees with wrists under shoulders and knees under hips.\n2. Brace the abdomen and keep the spine neutral.\n3. Keeping the working knee bent about 90°, drive the sole of the foot upward/backward by extending the hip.\n4. Stop before the low back arches or pelvis rotates.\n5. Lower the knee under control without resting fully between reps.\nCommon mistakes: lumbar arching, rotating the pelvis, swinging the leg, or trying to lift the foot excessively high.");
    MAP.put("Russian Twists", "1. Sit with knees bent, lean the torso back slightly and keep the spine long.\n2. Brace the abdomen; keep heels on the floor for easier control or lift them for more challenge.\n3. Rotate the ribcage and hands toward one hip while the pelvis stays mostly stable.\n4. Rotate through centre to the opposite side.\n5. Count both sides consistently and keep the movement controlled.\nCommon mistakes: moving only the arms, rounding the low back, swinging quickly, or letting knees sway excessively.");
    MAP.put("Scissor Kicks", "1. Lie on the back with legs straight and hands beside the body or under the pelvis for support.\n2. Brace and keep the low back gently pressed toward the floor.\n3. Lift both heels slightly, then alternate one leg up as the other lowers in a small scissor action.\n4. Keep knees mostly straight and movements controlled.\n5. Raise the legs higher if the low back begins to arch.\nCommon mistakes: low back lifting, very large uncontrolled kicks, holding the breath, or bending the knees excessively.");
    MAP.put("V-Ups", "1. Lie fully extended with arms overhead and legs straight.\n2. Brace the abdomen and simultaneously lift the straight legs and torso.\n3. Reach the hands toward the feet while balancing briefly on the pelvis.\n4. Keep the movement controlled rather than throwing the arms.\n5. Lower arms, torso and legs together without slamming into the floor.\nCommon mistakes: using momentum, bending the knees heavily, rounding the neck, or losing control on the descent.");
    MAP.put("Barbell Snatch", "1. Start with the bar over mid-foot, feet about hip width and a wide snatch grip.\n2. Brace, lift the bar from the floor with shoulders slightly in front of it, and keep it close to the legs.\n3. As the bar passes mid-thigh, extend hips, knees and ankles explosively while shrugging upward.\n4. Pull yourself under the rising bar and receive it overhead with arms locked and feet stable.\n5. Stand from the receiving position, then lower safely.\nCommon mistakes: bar looping away, early arm pull, jumping forward, soft elbows overhead, or losing spinal position.");
    MAP.put("Barbell Clean and Jerk", "1. Start with the bar over mid-foot, hands just outside the legs and back braced.\n2. Lift close to the body, then explosively extend hips, knees and ankles as the bar reaches the upper thigh.\n3. Pull under and receive the bar on the front shoulders in a front-squat rack, then stand.\n4. Dip straight down a few centimetres, drive through the legs and split or power under the bar.\n5. Lock the bar overhead, bring the feet together under control, then lower safely.\nCommon mistakes: early arm pull, bar looping forward, elbows slow in the clean, dip pitching forward, or pressing the jerk out with the arms.");
    MAP.put("Kettlebell Deadlift", "1. Place the kettlebell between the feet and stand about hip to shoulder width.\n2. Push the hips back and bend the knees enough to grip the handle with both hands.\n3. Brace the spine and keep the bell directly under the shoulders.\n4. Drive through the whole foot and extend the hips and knees to stand tall.\n5. Hinge back and bend the knees to return the bell softly to the floor.\nCommon mistakes: squatting too low, rounded back, bell drifting forward, or overextending at the top.");
    MAP.put("Kettlebell One Arm Press", "1. Clean the kettlebell into a secure rack position at one shoulder with wrist neutral.\n2. Stand tall, brace the abdomen and squeeze the glutes.\n3. Press the bell upward while keeping the forearm vertical and allowing the arm to rotate naturally.\n4. Finish with elbow straight, biceps near the ear and ribs controlled.\n5. Lower back to the rack under control.\nCommon mistakes: side-bending, over-arching the back, wrist collapsing, or letting the bell drift far forward.");
    MAP.put("Tuck Jumps", "1. Stand in an athletic stance with feet about hip width.\n2. Dip quickly at the hips and knees while swinging the arms back.\n3. Jump vertically and bring the knees upward toward the chest at the top of the jump.\n4. Extend the legs before landing and absorb the landing softly with hips and knees flexed.\n5. Reset balance before the next rep.\nCommon mistakes: landing with knees locked, pulling the knees up before sufficient jump height, knees collapsing inward, or continuing under severe fatigue.");
    MAP.put("Lying Back Extensions", "1. Lie face down with legs straight and hands beside the head or arms by the sides.\n2. Brace the abdomen lightly and keep the neck neutral.\n3. Lift the chest a small distance by extending the upper and mid back while keeping the pelvis in contact with the floor.\n4. Stop before excessive low-back compression or neck extension.\n5. Lower slowly until the chest returns to the floor.\nCommon mistakes: throwing the head back, lifting too high, using momentum, or holding the breath.");
    MAP.put("Triceps Dips on a Bench", "1. Sit on the edge of a stable bench, place hands beside the hips and slide the hips just forward of the edge.\n2. Keep shoulders down, chest open and feet planted; bend the knees to make the exercise easier.\n3. Bend the elbows straight backward and lower until around 90° or a comfortable shoulder depth.\n4. Press through the palms to straighten the elbows and lift back to the start.\n5. Keep the hips close to the bench throughout.\nCommon mistakes: hips drifting far forward, shoulders rolling inward, dropping too deep, or elbows flaring sideways.");
    MAP.put("Triceps Pushdown", "Start Position:\nStand facing a cable machine with a straight bar attachment at about chest height. Grasp the bar overhand, hands about shoulder-width apart. Keep your elbows close to your sides, core engaged and shoulders relaxed.\n\nMidpoint Position:\nPush the bar down by extending your elbows while keeping your upper arms close to your sides. Squeeze the triceps at the bottom, pause briefly, then return with control.");
    MAP.put("Superman", "Start Position:\nLie face down with your arms extended overhead, palms down. Keep your legs straight, toes on the floor and core engaged.\n\nMidpoint Position:\nSimultaneously lift your chest, arms and legs off the floor, squeezing your back muscles. Keep the neck neutral and avoid overextending the lower back. Pause, then lower with control.");
  }
  public static String get(String name) { return MAP.get(name); }
  private ExerciseInstructions() {}
}